// edge leaves, i.e. a loop head; widening a forward join such as a guard's edge into the body would
// throw away exactly the bound the guard just proved) - a join that would move a bound jumps
// instead (widening with thresholds): an upper bound that grew goes to the smallest immediate in
// the function that still covers it - the loop guard's own bound, typically, so `while i < 300`
// holds the counter at [0, 300] at the head and the guard narrows it to [0, 299] in the body - or
// to rg_max when none does; a lower bound that fell goes to 0; and a length generation the two
// paths disagree on becomes a fixed value for this block and identity, which no bound was ever
// stated against. All of these only lose facts, so they stay sound, and none moves twice, so a
// loop of any trip count converges in a few rounds.
fn rg_widen_gen(block: u64, id: u64) -> u64 {
